{% if needs_hosting %}

### Terraform derives from `terraform-modules`

`terraform/` wires pinned modules together; it does not declare AWS resources of
its own. Need something the modules don't cover? Add it to
[terraform-modules](https://github.com/NateDogg12501/terraform-modules), release
a tag, and bump every `?ref=` here together — one project pins one tag. The
`terraform` CI job fails on a `source =` that isn't a tag-pinned reference to
that repo.
{% if needs_datastore %}
The one hand-written resource here is expected, not a violation:
`aws_iam_role_policy.app_table_access` is glue between two modules — neither can
own a policy that spans both, so `lambda-web-app` exposes `lambda_role_name` for
exactly this. IAM joining two modules is the carve-out; anything a module could
own is not.
{% else %}
The only carve-out is IAM that joins two modules, and this project has none.
{% endif %}

State lives in S3 (`terraform/backend.tf`), in **one bucket shared across every
project generated from this template** — not a bucket this project owns.
`backend.tf`'s `key` is deliberately omitted and supplied at `terraform init
-backend-config="key={{ project_slug }}/<environment>/terraform.tfstate"`, since
it's the one thing that varies per apply and backend blocks can't interpolate.
Isolation from other projects sharing the bucket is this project's deploy role
being scoped to its own `{{ project_slug }}/*` prefix, not a separate bucket —
see `terraform/README.md`. Every apply also takes `-var="environment=..."`,
validated in `variables.tf` against the declared environment list; there's no
default, so an apply that doesn't say which environment fails immediately
instead of picking one silently.

### `deploy.yml` sequences stages; the stages hold the logic

`.github/workflows/deploy.yml` is dispatched with an `environment` input and
does nothing but call composite actions from `.github/actions/` in order. **If
you find yourself writing more than a few lines of shell in the workflow, it
belongs in a stage** — that is what keeps each one separately runnable and
separately testable, and the stage tests are the only place a check like
`capacity-gate` is ever seen to fail. Full contract in
[docs/deploy.md](docs/deploy.md).

Three things there are load-bearing and easy to undo by accident:

- **`terraform apply` applies a saved plan file.** A bare `apply` re-plans, so
  what runs stops being what was reviewed in the log above it.
- **`deploy-result.json` is uploaded on the failure path too.** It is read by
  something outside this repository, which is waiting on it either way.
- **`ensure-secrets` never rotates.** Regenerating `session_secret` on every
  deploy logs every user out on every ship.
- **A production deploy refuses the `ref` input, and that check is a security
  boundary.** Production's OIDC trust policy pins `refs/heads/main`, but it
  builds that pin from `github.ref` — the ref the run was *dispatched*
  against — while the `ref` input is read only by `actions/checkout`. Allowing
  both means dispatching against `main` for the pin and deploying something
  else, with AWS unable to tell. It looks like an arbitrary restriction on a
  convenience input; it is the only part of "only main deploys to production"
  that AWS cannot enforce itself.
{% if needs_datastore %}

### Staging starts empty, and that is the seed stage working

`seed` refuses to copy anything out of the production table until this project
provides `scripts/sanitize-seed.js` — the template can't know which attributes
are personal data, and a seed that guesses is a disclosure. Until that file
exists the stage warns, writes the reason into the job summary, and lets the
deploy carry on. **An empty staging table is the expected state of a new
project, not a bug to chase.** Start from `scripts/sanitize-seed.example.js`.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% if needs_datastore %}

### The local table schema and the Terraform table schema are two definitions of one thing

`backend/src/init-table.js` creates `{{ project_slug }}-items` in DynamoDB
Local with the same key schema `terraform/main.tf`'s `module "table"` declares
for real AWS — hash key `id`, string. Nothing keeps these in sync
automatically: change one and you have to change the other by hand, or local
and deployed silently diverge. `backend/src/dynamodb.js` is the only file that
constructs a DynamoDB client — every read and write goes through it, and it
switches between DynamoDB Local and real AWS on `AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_DYNAMODB`
alone, so the application code above it never branches on where it's running.
{% endif %}

## Testing conventions

vitest, one runner across the stack. Each package runs its own tests from its
own directory, against its own `vitest.config.js` — **don't delete those config
files because they look nearly empty.** Without one, vitest searches upward,
can pick up an unrelated config, and then reports "No test files found" while
exiting 0: a green run that tested nothing.

What ships with the scaffold, all of it real and all of it passing:
{% if needs_api %}

- `backend/test/` — vitest + supertest against `createApp()` from
  `src/app.js`. `src/index.js` is the only place that binds a port, so tests
  build an app without starting a server; keep that split when you add routes.
{% endif %}
{% if needs_datastore %}

- `backend/test/integration/` — real read/write assertions against the
  actual `dynamodb-local` container, not mocks. Excluded from plain `npm
  test` (see `backend/vitest.config.js`'s `exclude`) because they need the
  container running; `npm run test:integration` runs them by hand, and the
  `datastore` CI job runs them after bringing the stack up.
{% endif %}
{% if needs_ui %}

- `frontend/test/` — vitest with the jsdom environment, driving the real
  `index.html` rather than a fixture, so a change to the markup that breaks
  `app.js` fails here.
{% endif %}
{% if needs_hosting %}

- `.github/actions/*/test/` — the deploy stages, run by the `pipeline-stages`
  job. Every stage that talks to AWS takes its runner as a parameter
  (`lib/aws.js`), so pagination, retries and refusals are asserted against
  recorded calls instead of an account. The one that matters most is
  `capacity-gate` failing over its threshold: a gate whose failing path has
  never run is a gate that has only ever been observed to pass.
{% endif %}

